-- title:
--   Level automorphisms transport ends of the blown-up supersingular chart
-- statement:
--   **Setting.** Let $q$ and $\ell$ be primes with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$, let $M' \neq 0$ be a natural number with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`) and $\ell \mid M'$ (`hℓM'`). Let $L$ be a field of characteristic $0$ and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hζ`), and assume (`hι`) that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = \exp(2\pi i/q)$. Let $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ be the subgroup (`hH₁`) obtained as the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ given (`hK`) by [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion function field over $\mathbb{Q}$ attached to the congruence subgroup [`CohCarrier.GammaH (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ cut out inside $\Gamma_0(q^2M')$ by $H_1$.
--
--   Let $A$ be a Henselian discrete valuation ring which is an $L$-algebra with $L$ as its field of fractions and with algebraically closed residue field, such that $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K$, $j \neq 0$, be the element whose Laurent expansion is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $j$-series [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`). Let $\varpi$ generate $\mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hϖ`), and let $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q \cdot u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`). Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$. Let $y \subset C$ be a maximal ideal (`hy`) containing the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`) which is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of $\nu \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\nu$ has no non-zero $q$-torsion point.
--
--   Throughout, for $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ the phrase "$\tau$ is a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$" abbreviates [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29): for $\tau \in \operatorname{Aut}_L(K)$, every weight $k$, all modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ for [`CohCarrier.GammaH (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$, with the Laurent series of $p_g$ non-zero, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the ratio of those of $p_f$ and $p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = \exp(2\pi i/q)$, the product of the $\iota$-image of the expansion of $\tau x$ with the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$.
--
--   **The rigidification hypothesis `hArig`.** This hypothesis asserts, for every point $z$ of the two-chart integral model $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), every element $\varpi_z$ of the stalk of $X$ at $z$ equal to the germ at $z$ of the global section coming from $\varpi$ under the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and lying in the maximal ideal of that stalk, and every point $y'$ of `XFin A ↥K j` mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` and satisfying the same supersingularity condition as `hss` with $y$ replaced by $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, the existence of a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f - (X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1) \in (X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/\big(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f\,u\big)$, such that, writing $\mathrm{toC}$ for the map from the stalk to its completion, $\mathrm{mkS}$ for the quotient map onto $S$, and $\mathrm{germY}$ for the canonical homomorphism $C \to \mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ through the open immersion `ιFin`, the following seven clauses hold: (a) $e$ carries the germ of each $a \in A$ to the constant $C(\sigma a)$; (b) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$; (c) for such $\gamma$ with $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$ and such $\tau$ preserving $C$, the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$; (d) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, there are $\theta \in \operatorname{Aut}(S)$, $c \in W$ and $M \in M_2(W)$ such that $\theta$ intertwines the composite $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ with the restriction of $\tau$, $\theta$ fixes all constants, $\theta(X_{jj}) \equiv \sum_{ii} M_{ii\,jj} X_{ii}$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ii\,jj} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ii\,jj}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$, moreover $c \equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$, and $c \not\equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (e) for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1, P_2$ of $S$, each not containing both classes of $X_0$ and $X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and each containing an element $C(a_i)X_0 + C(b_i)X_1 + h_i$ with $h_i \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ along $e \circ \mathrm{toC}$ differ; (f) for a prime $P$ of $S$ not containing both classes of $X_0, X_1$, containing that of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and containing $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ satisfies $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a) \in e^{-1}P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$; (g) the series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (the $j$-series with exponents scaled by $q$ via `qExpand L q`) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with the difference of that element and $a_0$ in $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form is a unit at every pair $(a,b) \in W^2$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, such that $e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,(\cdot)))$ of that difference is the class of $h$.
--
--   **The chosen point and its chart.** Fixed are a point $z$ of $X$, the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ at $z$ (`hϖz`) lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hz`), a point $y'$ of `XFin A ↥K j` with `ιFin` mapping $y'$ to $z$ (`hy'`), supersingular in the sense of `hss'`, and with $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal} = y$ (`hy'y`). Fixed further are an explicit witness $(W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1)$ of the kind produced by `hArig`: $W_1$ a complete discrete valuation ring, $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ with $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$ (`hσ₁`), units $u_1, v_1$ (`hu₁`, `hv₁`), $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S := W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for this data exactly the conjunction of the seven clauses (a)–(g) above.
--
--   **The blown-up chart.** $J$ is the ideal of $C$ given (`hJ`) as the infimum of all ideals of the form $\mathrm{comap}$ along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ of the contraction along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, the infimum being over all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$. $B$ is the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ given (`hB`) by adjoining to $C$ all $x \in K$ with $x\varpi_t \in J$. $W$ is a valuation subring of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`).
--
--   The hypotheses on these objects are: `hR1` ($C \le B$, and every element of $K$ is a ratio $g/h$ with $g, h \in B$, $h \neq 0$); `hR2` ($A \to B$ formally smooth and of finite presentation, and $B/\varpi B$ of Krull dimension at most $1$); `hR3`, five clauses, namely that an element of $L$ lies in $W$ exactly when it comes from $A$, that $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that for $b \in C$ one has $b \in y$ if and only if the image of $b$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_W$, and that $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at that centre ($f \in W$ iff $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B$ and $h \notin \mathfrak{m}_W$); and `hEQ`, five clauses (summarised here), namely that for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel the elements whose image lies in $\mathfrak{m}_W$, compatible with $A \to \mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, and equivariant for all $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W$ through an element $c \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,\gamma, c)$ in the subgroup [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)c^{q+1}$) acting by [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325), with $c \neq 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; that $B$ is stable under all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; that every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached from the centre by a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; that an element of $B$ all of whose such translates lie in $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is divisible by $\varpi$; and that $W$ is stable under any level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, which preserves $y$.
--
--   **The end $O$ and the automorphism.** $O$ is a subring of $K$ which is an *end* of the blown-up chart in the sense of `hO`: there are $a \in J$ with non-zero image in $K$ and, setting $B_a$ to be the $A$-subalgebra obtained by adjoining to $C$ all $x \in K$ with $xa \in J$, a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$ ($f \in O$ iff $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$), every $b \in y$ whose image lies in $O$ is a non-unit there, and $B$ is not contained in $O$. Finally, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ (`hγ`), $\tau$ is a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ (`hτ`), and $W$ is $\tau$-stable in the sense that $f \in W \iff \tau f \in W$ (`hτW`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists a subring $O'$ of $K$ with the following three properties.
--
--   1.
--
--   $O'$ is again an end: there are $a \in J$ with non-zero image in $K$ and, with $B_a$ formed from $a$ as above, a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ such that $f \in O'$ holds exactly for $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B_a$ and $h \notin P$, every $b \in y$ whose image lies in $O'$ is a non-unit of $O'$, and $B$ is not contained in $O'$.
--
--   2.
--
--   For every $f \in K$: $f \in O$ if and only if $\tau f \in O'$.
--
--   3.
--
--   For all prime ideals $\mathfrak{p}, \mathfrak{p}'$ of $C$, each containing the image of $\varpi$ and satisfying $\mathfrak{p} \le y$, $\mathfrak{p} \neq y$ (respectively $\mathfrak{p}' \le y$, $\mathfrak{p}' \neq y$): if $\mathfrak{p}$ is traced by a prime of $O$, that is, there is a prime $\mathfrak{q}$ of $O$ such that the image of $\varpi$ lies in $\mathfrak{q}$ whenever it lies in $O$, some $b \in y$ has image outside $\mathfrak{q}$ whenever its image lies in $O$, and for every $c \in C$ whose image lies in $O$ one has $c \in \mathfrak{p}$ if and only if that image lies in $\mathfrak{q}$; and if $\tau$ carries $\mathfrak{p}$ to $\mathfrak{p}'$, in the sense that for every $c \in C$ with $\tau c \in C$ one has $c \in \mathfrak{p}$ if and only if $\tau c \in \mathfrak{p}'$; then $\mathfrak{p}'$ is traced by a prime of $O'$: there is a prime $\mathfrak{q}$ of $O'$ containing the image of $\varpi$ whenever that image lies in $O'$, such that some $b \in y$ has image outside $\mathfrak{q}$ whenever its image lies in $O'$, and such that for every $c \in C$ whose image lies in $O'$ one has $c \in \mathfrak{p}'$ if and only if that image lies in $\mathfrak{q}$.
--
--   At a supersingular point of the integral model of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ the special fibre of the blow-up along the ideal $J$ acquires several ends (Igusa-type branches); this statement says that a level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which stabilises the exceptional valuation ring $W$ carries each end $O$ to an end $O'$, and transports the branch data traced on the chart algebra $C$ along with it. It feeds the counting of ends and the localisation description of the blown-up chart used in the level-one auxiliary analysis of the special fibre.
